WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.538
Alpine photography starts before the summit catches light. I walk the ridge in blue hour and choose one camera position while the valley is still in shadow.

00:00:10.538 --> 00:00:21.340
For alpine photography, side light is the first thing I look for. Side light catches the limestone edges while the rock behind them stays dark.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:30.274
A long lens helps separate one ridge from the next. I keep the nearest pine trees in the frame so the layers have a sense of scale.

00:00:30.274 --> 00:00:40.152
Watch the low cloud. When low cloud moves through the valley, the middle ridge disappears for a moment and the outline of the summit becomes cleaner.

00:00:40.152 --> 00:00:51.150
Once the side light reaches the highest peak, I check the histogram. I lower the exposure just enough to hold detail in the brightest patch of limestone.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:01:04.172
Before I leave, I make one wider frame. Alpine photography rewards waiting. A long lens describes the mountain, but the wider view shows what the morning felt like.
